      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/Jenkins,_Kentucky",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "A narrow‑gauge railroad was first built over Pine Mountain to bring supplies, then the Sandy Valley and Elkhorn line stretched 30 miles to link Jenkins to broader markets.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10,
        17
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jenkins%2C_Kentucky",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "By 1916, Consolidation Coal had erected hundreds of company homes, nine sawmills, two brickyards, schools, a hospital, and recreational facilities for miners.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7,
        23,
        24
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/Jenkins,_Kentucky",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "During the 1920s and 1930s, Jenkins swelled to roughly 10,000 residents as coal production from multiple mines reached its peak.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        14
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/Jenkins,_Kentucky",
